## Why This Is Asked

Probes on-device NLP, privacy, and latency trade-offs in edge deployments typical of Cloudflare/Uber-scale products.

## Key Concepts

- On-device inference with deterministic latency
- Regex-based redaction plus tiny ML for edge cases
- Multilingual and code-switching handling
- Privacy controls and local data labeling

## Code Example

```javascript
const redact = (text) => {
  const emails = /[\w.+-]+@[\w.-]+\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}/g;
  const phones = /(\+?\d[\d\s\-\(\)]{7,}\d)/g;
  let out = text.replace(emails, "[REDACTED_EMAIL]");
  out = out.replace(phones, "[REDACTED_PHONE]");
  out = out.replace(/\b[A-Z][a-z]+ [A-Z][a-z]+\b/g, "[REDACTED_NAME]");
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure and guarantee 100 ms latency across devices?
- How would you handle false positives and user-triggered corrections without server contact?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-world enterprise risk monitoring requires multilingual understanding with code-switching while preserving privacy. It also tests ability to design federated updates, per-tenant governance, and secure rollback in production.

## Key Concepts
- Federated learning and differential privacy for cross-tenant training
- Multilingual handling with code-switching and low-resource languages
- Real-time alerting vs. post-hoc review pipeline
- Explainability and per-message risk scoring
- Drift detection and secure rollout/rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
function riskScore(tokens, model) {
  // placeholder: real inference would run a lightweight classifier
  // returns 0..1 risk probability for the message
  const score = model.predict(tokens);
  return Math.max(0, Math.min(1, score));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate drift across tenants and under new vocabularies?
- What security controls ensure privacy while enabling per-tenant explainability?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design latency-aware, privacy-preserving, multilingual systems that justify decisions rather than just detect. Emphasizes RAG, policy integration, and drift handling across many languages.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) vs pure classifiers
- On-device first-pass with quantized transformers
- Privacy: differential privacy, encrypted policy KB, secure inference
- Multilingual handling and code-switching
- Drift detection, rollout strategies, rollback plans

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo architecture sketch
class Detector {
  constructor(){ /* load on-device model */ }
  async predict(msg){
    const lang = identifyLanguage(msg);
    const local = await runOnDevice(msg, lang);
    const refs = await fetchPolicyRefs(msg, lang); // retrieval
    const rationale = buildRAG(refs, local);
    const cloudOut = await cloudInfer(msg, rationale);
    return { label: cloudOut.label, rationale };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evaluate policy-ref quality and latency separately?
- How would you handle a new language with minimal data and ensure privacy material?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to enable rapid domain adaptation with privacy guarantees in a real-time environment, emphasizing latency constraints, privacy preservation, and robust rollback mechanisms.

## Key Concepts

- Parameter-efficient adapters (LoRA/Adapters) for domain adaptation
- On-device fine-tuning with privacy guarantees (DP or federated learning)
- Streaming drift detection and safe rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: load base model, attach adapter for domain, fine-tune on small labeled set
function adaptDomain(model, domain, labeledData) {
  const adapter = new LoRAAdapter(model, { rank: 4, alpha: 8 });
  adapter.fineTune(labeledData, { privacy: 'DP-SGD', epochs: 3 });
  return adapter;
}
```
